          %title
          %h1#idea Idea
          %p
            One can naturally think of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cospan"} cospan
            as the abstraction of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+object"} interval object
            cospan models the standard topological interval
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]
            regarded as a cobordism from pt to pt. The
            %em cospan cotrace
            on the interval glues the two ends of the interval together to produce a
            %em circle
            regarded as a cospan from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset
            to itself.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} concrete dual
            of a cospan, obtained by mapping it into some target object, is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span"} span
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoidification"} groupoidification
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+function+theory"} geometric function theory
            can be interpreted as a generalized linear map. On such a generalized linear map, there is a notion of
            = succeed "," do
              %em trace
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span+trace"} span trace
          %p
            The
            %em cospan cotrace
            is the concept dual to that: the image of the cotrace of a cospan under mapping it into a target object is the span trace of the result of mapping the original cospan to that target object.
          %h1#definition Definition
          %p For
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi T
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mi in
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo ↖
                            %mi out
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Σ
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; T
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}^{in}\nearrow &amp;&amp; \nwarrow^{out}
                  \\\
                  \\Sigma &amp;&amp;&amp;&amp; \Sigma
                  }
          %p
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cospan"} cospan
            with identical left and right index object
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %strong cospan cotrace
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi cotr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cotr(T)
            is the composite of the result
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi T
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mrow
                              %mi in
                              %mo ⊔
                              %mi out
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↖
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Σ
                          %mo ⊔
                          %mi Σ
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; T
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}^{in \sqcup out}\nearrow &amp;&amp; \nwarrow
                  \\\
                  \\Sigma \sqcup \Sigma &amp;&amp;&amp;&amp; \emptyset
                  }
          %p of dualizing one leg of the cospan with the cospan
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
