          %h2#definition_for_multicospans Definition for multi-cospans
          %p
            More generally, the trace of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multi-cospan"} multi-cospan
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            identical of its index objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            is the composite with the multi-cospan
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi Σ
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mi Id
                          %mo ↗
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                            %mi Id
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋯
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Σ
                        %mtd
                          %mi Σ
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋯
                        %mtd
                          %mi Σ
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp; \Sigma
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}^{Id}\nearrow \uparrow^{Id} &amp; \cdots
                  \\\
                  \\Sigma &amp; \Sigma &amp; \cdots &amp; \Sigma &amp; \cdots
                  }
          %h1#examples Examples
          %h2#cotracing_topological_interval_to_circle Cotracing topological interval to circle
          %p
            Let the ambient category be
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I = [0,1]
            be the standard topological interval and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e := [0,\epsilon]
            be a small interval, for some
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \lt \epsilon \lt 1/2
            – to be thought here as a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/collar"} collar
            of the point
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi pt
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pt
          %p Let
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi I
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mrow
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo ↖
                            %mrow
                              %mn 1
                              %mo −
                              %mi ϵ
                              %mo +
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi e
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; I
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}\nearrow &amp;&amp; \nwarrow^{1-\epsilon+(-)}
                  \\\
                  e &amp;&amp;&amp;&amp; e
                  }
          %p be the interval regarded as a collared cobordisms from the point to the point. Its cotrace, the pushout
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi cotr
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi I
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo ←
                        %mtd
                          %mi I
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                            %mrow
                              %mi in
                              %mo ⊔
                              %mi out
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi e
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ←
                            %mrow
                              %mi Id
                              %mo ⊔
                              %mi Id
                        %mtd
                          %mi e
                          %mo ⊔
                          %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  cotr(I) &amp;\leftarrow&amp; I
                  \\\
                  \\uparrow &amp;&amp; \uparrow^{in \sqcup out}
                  \\\
                  e &amp;\stackrel{Id \sqcup Id}{\leftarrow}&amp; e \sqcup e
                  }
          %p is the result of gluing the ends of the interval to each other, i.e. the circle
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi cotr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  cotr(I) = S^1
                  \\,.
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              Marco Grandis,
              %em Collared cospans, cohomotopy and TQFT (Cospans in Algebraic Topology II)
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.dima.unige.it/~grandis/wCub2.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %h2#cotracing_category_interval_object_to_the_natural_numbers Cotracing category interval object to the natural numbers
          %p
            Let the ambient category be
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi a
                  %mo →
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I = \{a \to b\}
            be the standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+object"} interval object
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi pt
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pt = \{\bullet\}
            be the terminal category.
          %p Let
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi I
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mrow
                              %mi pt
                              %mo ↦
                              %mi a
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo ↖
                            %mrow
                              %mi pt
                              %mo ↦
                              %mi b
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi pt
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi pt
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; I
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}^{pt \mapsto a}\nearrow &amp;&amp; \nwarrow^{pt \mapsto b}
                  \\\
                  pt &amp;&amp;&amp;&amp; pt
                  }
          %p
            be the standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+object"} interval object
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            regarded in the standard way as a cospan from the point to the point.
          %p Dualizing it to
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi I
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mrow
                              %mi in
                              %mo ⊔
                              %mi out
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo ↖
                            %mrow
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi pt
                          %mo ⊔
                          %mi pt
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; I
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}^{in \sqcup out}\nearrow &amp;&amp; \nwarrow^{}
                  \\\
                  pt \sqcup pt &amp;&amp;&amp;&amp; \emptyset
                  }
          %p corresponds to thinking of it as a “bent interval”
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi pt
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↙
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi pt
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  pt
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \searrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \swarrow
                  \\\
                  pt
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p Accordingly, the co-span
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi pt
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mrow
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo ↖
                            %mrow
                              %mi Id
                              %mo ⊔
                              %mi Id
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ∅
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi pt
                          %mo ⊔
                          %mi pt
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; pt
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}^{}\nearrow &amp;&amp; \nwarrow^{Id \sqcup Id}
                  \\\
                  \\emptyset &amp;&amp;&amp;&amp; pt \sqcup pt
                  }
          %p can be thought of as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi pt
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↗
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↖
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi pt
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp; pt
                  \\\
                  \\nearrow
                  \\\
                  \\nwarrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp; pt
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p Gluing these two arcs together yields the cotrace, the pushout
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi cotr
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi I
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo ←
                        %mtd
                          %mi I
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                            %mrow
                              %mi in
                              %mo ⊔
                              %mi out
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi pt
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ←
                            %mrow
                              %mi Id
                              %mo ⊔
                              %mi Id
                        %mtd
                          %mi pt
                          %mo ⊔
                          %mi pt
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  cotr(I) &amp;\leftarrow&amp; I
                  \\\
                  \\uparrow &amp;&amp; \uparrow^{in \sqcup out}
                  \\\
                  pt &amp;\stackrel{Id \sqcup Id}{\leftarrow}&amp; pt \sqcup pt
                  }
                  \\,,
          %p
            which is the result of gluing the ends of the interval object to each other, which here is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/skeleton"} skeleton
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+category"} fundamental category
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+space"} directed circle
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↖
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↙
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ←
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; \rightarrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \nearrow &amp;&amp; \searrow
                  \\\
                  \\uparrow &amp;&amp;&amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \nwarrow &amp;&amp; \swarrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \leftarrow
                  }
                  \\,,
          %p
            namely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            of natural numbers, regarded as a one-object category:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi cotr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo •
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi n
                  %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi n
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  cotr(I) = \mathbf{B} \mathbb{N} = \{\bullet \stackrel{n}{\to} \bullet | n \in \mathbb{N}\}
                  \\,.
          %p
            If instead we start with the standard interval object in groupoids,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi I
                    %mi inv
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi a
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ≃
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I_{inv} = \{a \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} b\}
            with the nontrivial morphism from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            being an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/skeleton"} skeleton
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid"} fundamental groupoid
            of the ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological circle
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi cotr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi I
                    %mi inv
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo •
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi n
                  %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi n
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  cotr(I_{inv}) = \mathbf{B} \mathbb{Z} = \{\bullet \stackrel{n}{\to} \bullet | n \in \mathbb{Z}\}
                  \\,.
          %h1#remarks Remarks
          %ul
            %li
              The dual notion is that of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span+trace"} span trace
          %h1#references References
          %p While the concept is obvious, it is apparently (?) not discussed yet in the (young) literature on the subject. On the blog the concept was mentioned in
          %ul
            %li
              Urs Schreiber,
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2008/05/hopkinslurie_on_baezdolan.html#c021537"} (co)-traces
        .revisedby
